---

```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
The fault lies in router6's iptables FORWARD chain, which contains three injected DROP
rules that silently discard all forwarded ICMP (proto 0), TCP, and UDP traffic arriving
on eth2 — the WAN interface connecting router6 to router3 (10.50.1.0/24 link). This
effectively isolates Location C (the mswitch2 area: PC15–PC18 on 172.18.51.0/24 and
172.18.50.0/24) from the rest of the network. Any packet from Location A (PC4/PC5,
172.16.10.x) destined for Location C must traverse eth2 inbound at router6, where it is
dropped. Conversely, even when a Location C host initiates traffic outward to Location A,
the echo reply from Location A must return inbound on eth2 and is equally dropped, making
both directions fail. The ICMP DROP rule (rule 4, in=eth2) shows a hit counter of exactly
6 — matching the two 3-packet ping tests run during this investigation (PC4→PC15 and
PC4→PC16). The traceroute from PC4 to PC15 confirms the path reaches router6 (hop 5 =
10.50.1.2) and goes silent immediately after. Router6 can reach PC15 locally (no FORWARD
chain involved), and all RIP daemons and interfaces are healthy. The one remaining ACCEPT
rule (TCP/80 from 172.16.10.0/24 to 172.18.51.1) is intentional policy and should be
preserved. Root cause: three iptables DROP rules were injected into router6's FORWARD
chain blocking all forwarded traffic on eth2 (ICMP, TCP, UDP). Corrective fix: remove
the three DROP rules from the FORWARD chain on router6:
  iptables -D FORWARD -p icmp -i eth2 -j DROP
  iptables -D FORWARD -p udp -i eth2 -j DROP
  iptables -D FORWARD -p tcp -i eth2 -j DROP
=== END ===
```